    "title": {
     "en": "Making rakhis"
    },
    "text": {
     "en": "Gopal and Dhara's Atya is visiting for Raksha Bandhan. Each rakhi needs 1 flower, 2 threads and 4 beads. They want to make 5 rakhis."
    }
   },
   {
    "id": "explain",
    "type": "explanation",
    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "times",
     "groups of",
     "repeated addition",
     "multiply"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "It is Raksha Bandhan and Gopal and Dhara are making rakhis.\nEach rakhi needs 1 flower. For 5 rakhis, 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5 flowers.\nThis can also be said as 5 times 1, or 5 × 1 = 5.\nEach rakhi needs 2 threads. For 5 rakhis, 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 10 threads. That is 5 × 2 = 10.\nEach rakhi needs 4 beads. 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 20 beads, so 5 × 4 = 20.\nRepeated addition of the same number is called multiplication. We write it with the sign ×.\nFor 10 rakhis we need 10 flowers, 20 threads and 40 beads. Try it by adding or by skip counting."
    }
   },
